{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\n\nThe 2026-08-05/06 codebase-health audit (`.joe-janitor/report-2026-08-06.md`) found a\nuser-facing Jira reconciler surface that leaked internals and coupled core behavior to GitHub\nActions. Six verbs mixed live, dead, and provider-specific vocabulary; `--mode` fused mutation\nlimits with run permission; destructive repair shelled out to `gh`; and the GHA workflow contained\na duplicate 106-line CAS push loop. This epic replaces those mechanics with one provider-neutral,\nPython-first bridge surface while retaining every real legacy entrypoint needed by existing\nworkflows and environments.\n\n## Decisions\n\n- The canonical noun is `bridge`; `jira` was rejected because other trackers are planned.\n- `bridge preview` and `bridge sync` are primary. `reconcile` remains a compatibility adapter and\n  retains its safe dry-run default rather than aliasing argument-less, mutating `bridge sync`.\n- Canonical verbs are setup, check-access, fsck, status, preview, sync, pause, and resume.\n  `bridge-status` remains a compatibility entrypoint; audited-dead `purge-bridge` stays removed.\n- Canonical exit codes are 0/1/2; legacy adapters retain historical numeric translations.\n- The only operational flags are `--max-changes`, `--only`, `--except`, and `--max-age`; their help\n  is written without internal vocabulary.\n- CLI, MCP, and Python-library surfaces share the bridge functionality.\n- Packaged Python owns orchestration; GitHub Actions, Jenkins, and GitLab files are thin declarative\n  wrappers. Every change preserves old vocabulary and migrates in-repo callers in the same\n  shippable unit.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] `rebar bridge <verb>` is canonical for setup, check-access, fsck, status, preview, sync,\n      pause, and resume; `doctor` was never a legacy bridge command and is not added.\n- [x] Current workflows and docs use canonical bridge vocabulary. `reconcile` and `bridge-status`\n      retain old safe contracts. `purge-bridge` remains removed only because repository and\n      deployment inventories prove no consumer; release notes record every mapping.\n- [x] Every shipped flag has plain-language help and a CI, local, or troubleshooting use.\n- [x] Canonical bridge exits are 0/1/2 and the CI whitelist is gone; legacy adapters retain\n      historical codes for old workflows and environments.\n- [x] Pause is remote-persisted, carries a reason, and cannot wedge a mixed-version fleet.\n- [x] rebar core requires no `gh` binary for any documented operation.\n- [x] The bridge runs locally, on GitHub Actions, Jenkins, GitLab CI, and bare environments.\n- [x] The workflow CAS push loop is deleted and `src/rebar/_store/push.py` owns the behavior.\n- [x] The former broken `docs/user-guide.md` `--dry-run` example is covered against recurrence.\n\n## Rollback and blast radius\n\n- Default inversion is highest risk: no-argument `reconcile` stays dry-run while no-argument\n  `bridge sync` mutates. Real-entrypoint parity tests prevent a naive alias.\n- Surface changes are additive and independently revertible; both pre- and post-change binaries\n  accept legacy vocabulary, with no data migration.\n- The persistent pause format lands reader-first (child 1104): readers accept old and new blobs\n  before writers emit the additive format, and retained `gated_mode` keeps downgrade safe.\n- Core push behavior lands before workflow-loop deletion (child 1c1b); the workflow remains\n  independently revertible.\n- The deliberate `bridge fsck` output change (child 030f) is recorded in release notes; its sole\n  in-repo consumer uses only `binding_drift`.\n\n## Portability acceptance criteria\n\n- [x] A scrubbed-environment contract covers local, GitHub Actions, Jenkins, GitLab CI, and bare\n      execution identically, failing on vendor-variable reads or required `gh` invocation.\n- [x] Full-depth execution succeeds; a shallow clone is rejected with a named error (bug f193).\n- [x] `Jenkinsfile` and `.gitlab-ci.yml` are committed files referenced by the docs.\n- [x] The GHA artifact is the production workflow exercised every 20 minutes.\n- [x] All provider configs share one code-derived exit table, secret-name set, and timeout.\n- [x] Jenkins shell execution uses a single-quoted Groovy body so secrets reach the shell without\n      Groovy interpolation.\n- [x] `.gitlab-ci.yml` validates offline against a vendored official schema whose immutable source\n      revision, URL, retrieval date, dialect, and checksum are recorded. `jsonschema` remains the\n      existing validator dependency.\n- [x] Pinned shellcheck validates extracted bash bodies from all three providers. It is a required\n      test-harness tool, not a runtime dependency or an extension of the polyglot bespoke lint.\n- [x] CI provisions the pinned shellcheck version, and a missing binary produces a named failure\n      rather than a skip.\n- [x] Residual risks are dispositioned on this epic instead of deferred to future adopters."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786431878997263001,"uuid":"dc93f93e-2a9d-4523-8c55-00cf4487bbf3"}